{"author":{"id":"127a58c7f014820991be95805df42525db12c92e2d7319d04d4df105a9aa3444"},"ops":[{"type":3,"timestamp":1788200714,"nonce":"H0bqVLOLXKnRDhR4QMgZTD+b4l4=","message":"VERIFICATION (backlog verification pass, git-bug 2f2796e, run fbb2c59)\n\nCONFIRMED — the defect is real and is exactly the `pawns` shape. Three additions,\none of which is a spec ambiguity that would otherwise cost a round trip.\n\nWHAT I CONFIRMED BY READING\n\n1. The addressable-list-ordered-by-a-live-score claim. `Source/AutoRimmer/ThingVerbs.cs`,\n   `ThingVerbs.Rollups`, `if (detail)` branch: the list is sorted\n   `ThingAttention(b,..).CompareTo(ThingAttention(a,..))` with a `thingIDNumber`\n   tie-break, cut at `detailCap`, emitted as `d[\"things\"]` with `[\"id\"] = t.thingIDNumber`\n   per line and `d[\"things_order\"] = \"attention-desc\"`. Addressable, attention-ordered,\n   no second order fact, no `attention_rank`. CONFIRMED.\n\n2. The score really does move at read time — and the moving term is NOT the one\n   the analogy to `pawns` implies. `ThingVerbs.ThingAttention` is\n   `100000*forbidden + Math.Max(0, 100 - hp_pct)*100 + Math.Min(stackCount, 1000)`.\n   `forbidden` only moves on an action. The two live terms are **hit points**\n   (deterioration ticks HP down on anything unroofed/outdoors, so an item at 74%\n   and one at 73% trade places when either crosses a percentage point) and\n   **`stackCount`**, which a hauler changes every time it merges or splits a\n   stack — with no player action at all. So the mechanism differs from mood but\n   the consequence is identical. CONFIRMED that it moves; the specific pace is\n   INFERRED from the formula, not measured.\n\n3. \"The fix is already designed\" — CONFIRMED and it is shipped, in\n   `Source/AutoRimmer/PawnVerbs.cs` (not `PawnActs.cs`; `PawnActs.PawnList` is the\n   action-side list the DESIGN entry contrasts against). The exact contract to\n   port, so a worker does not have to re-derive it:\n\n     - `private const string OrderById = \"id\"; OrderByAttention = \"attention\";`\n       — the ARGUMENT words are `id` and `attention`.\n     - `[\"order\"]` publishes `\"id-asc\"` or `\"attention-desc\"` — the FIELD values\n       differ from the argument words. Both halves matter for \"same vocabulary\".\n     - `[\"selected_by\"] = \"attention-desc\"` unconditionally.\n     - an unknown `order` is a `VerbArgsException`, never a silent fallback\n       (`$\"unknown order {order} ({OrderWords})\"`), with the stated reasoning\n       that a typo quietly returning the other order is the worst outcome.\n     - `attention_rank` is the 0-based position under attention order, computed\n       from the ranking BEFORE the cut and attached per line.\n     - the re-sort is `kept.Sort(...)` — survivors only, after the cap.\n     - the class comment carries the honesty note worth porting verbatim in\n       substance: `order:\"id\"` stabilises the SEQUENCE, not the MEMBERSHIP; on a\n       list longer than `cap` the surviving SET still moves, so a caller wanting\n       a durable handle raises `cap` past `total` or holds the `id`.\n\nADDITION 1 — **THE ACCEPTANCE IS AMBIGUOUS ABOUT WHICH LIST, AND THE OBVIOUS\nREADING IS WRONG.** `things` returns TWO lists and they are not the same kind of\nobject:\n\n  * `rollups` — one entry PER DEF (`ThingVerbs.Roll`), sorted `attention-desc` by\n    `ThingVerbs.Attention(Roll)`. Its lines carry `def`/`label`/`count`, and\n    **no `id`, because a rollup has no `thingIDNumber` to carry.** It is already\n    addressable, by `def`, which is a stable key. Re-sorting it \"by thingIDNumber\n    ascending\" is not implementable and would not be an improvement.\n  * `things` — the per-thing detail list, only present under `detail:true`.\n    THIS is the addressable list the issue is about.\n\n  Read literally (\"`things` emits by `thingIDNumber` ascending by default\"), a\n  worker will try to reorder both. Recommendation: scope the port to the detail\n  list; for `rollups`, if anything is owed it is a `def-asc` option and an\n  `attention_rank`, and the case for even that is weak. Worth deciding on-issue\n  before the work starts.\n\nADDITION 2 — THERE IS A THIRD INSTANCE IN THE SAME FILE AND THE ISSUE DOES NOT\nNAME IT: `fires`. `ThingVerbs.FireScan` emits `[\"list\"]` where each line carries\n`[\"id\"] = t.thingIDNumber`, sorted by `size + (atHome ? 0f : 10f)` descending\n(`[\"order\"] = \"outside-home-then-size-desc\"`), cut at `FireCap = 12`. `Fire.fireSize`\ngrows every tick while the fire burns, so this is an addressable list ordered by\na value that moves FASTER than either mood or hit points. It is reachable two\nways — the `fires` verb and `things` own `data[\"fire\"] = FireScan(map)` — so a\nport scoped to \"the `things` verb\" hits it by accident on one route and not the\nother. Decide deliberately. (My read: it deserves the same treatment. An agent\ndispatching firefighters is precisely a caller holding handles, and the cap of\n12 makes the membership churn too.)\n\nADDITION 3 — WHAT IS **NOT** AN INSTANCE, so the audit does not overreach. I\ngrepped every `[\"order\"]` publication in `Source/AutoRimmer/`:\n\n  DigestVerb (pawn list)   \"attention-desc\"  — NOT addressable: the line carries\n                             `name`, not `id`. It is a summary, position IS the\n                             message. Leave it.\n  PlaceVerbs `rooms`       \"proper-indoor-then-size-desc\" — score is indoor-ness\n                             plus cell count; moves only on construction. Not\n                             this bug. (Separate, unrelated hazard worth knowing:\n                             a `Room.ID` is not stable across a region rebuild,\n                             so it is a weak handle for a different reason.)\n  PlaceVerbs `zones`       \"cells-desc\" — moves only on player action.\n  PlaceVerbs `areas`       \"list-priority-desc\" — `Area.ListPriority` is fixed.\n  ColonyVerbs              \"id-asc\" already; \"started-then-cheapest\" and\n                             \"shortfall-desc\" are not id-addressable.\n  PawnSerializer           \"offset-desc-head-and-tail\", \"defdatabase\",\n                             \"natural-priority-desc\" — none addressable by index.\n  InteractionVerbs         \"timeout-then-choice-then-arrival\" — see 3.5.\n\n  So: two real instances (`things` detail, `fires`), one already fixed (`pawns`),\n  and nothing else. That is the evidence for the rule being general, and it is\n  also the ceiling — the sweep is done, no third file needs auditing.\n\nON THE ACCEPTANCE — testable as written, with one gap\n\n- \"Two reads with a score change in between (and no add/remove) return the same\n  entries in the same order\" is only true when `things_total \u003c= detail_cap`.\n  Above the cap, membership still moves with the score by design (that is 2.6),\n  so the check as written FAILS on a large map for the correct reason. Pin the\n  fixture: either read with `detail_cap` above `things_total`, or assert on the\n  intersection. `pawns` own class comment already makes this distinction; the\n  acceptance here does not inherit it.\n- \"demonstrate with a capped read that the urgent item is present\" is\n  straightforward: forbid one item, read with `cap:1` / `detail_cap:1`, assert\n  the forbidden one survived (its `+100000` term dominates).\n- Bench calls the orchestrator would run: `things {detail:true, detail_cap:N}`\n  twice across a hauling tick; `things {detail:true, detail_cap:1}` with one\n  forbidden stack on the map; `things {detail:true, order:\"attention\"}` for the\n  explicit view. All observer-only, no fixture build needed beyond a stockpile\n  with mixed stacks and one forbidden item.\n\nWHAT I AM INFERRING, NOT CONFIRMING\n\n- That `stackCount` churn from hauling is frequent enough to reorder a real\n  colony read. The formula makes it possible; I have not observed it.\n- That `Fire.fireSize` grows monotonically each tick. I read the field being\n  used, not `Fire.Tick`.","files":null}]}